  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nTEMPERATURES & WIND:\n\nTHIS EVENING--EARLY SUNDAY...A tight pressure gradient and adequate \nmixing behind a strong cold front will support strong/gusty \nnorthwest winds spreading southeast across the region this evening \nthrough early Sunday. The strongest winds will likely occur within a \n2-4 hour period immediately in wake of the surging cold front \nbetween about 9 PM to 3 AM, and roughly west-southwest of a line \nextending from Lincoln to McPherson to El Dorado, where model \nsoundings support periodic gusts up to around 50-55 mph. We expanded \nthe wind advisory another 1-2 rows of counties northeast, with \ntoday's shift possibly needing to expand another row to the east-\nnortheast.\n\nTemperature-wise, with the exception of seasonably cool weather \nSunday, seasonably mild to above average temperatures are expected \nthe next several days, especially next week. Highs in the 50s are \nlikely, with some 60s probable Wednesday and Thursday ahead of the \nnext cold front late next week. In wake of this cold front, a return \nto more seasonably temperatures is likely by next Friday-Saturday.\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES: \n\nTHIS EVENING--EARLY SUNDAY...A potent shortwave and an associated \nstrong/deep cold front approaching from the northwest will support \nan area of widespread rain spreading southeast across the region \nthis evening through early Sunday. Rainfall amounts will be tempered \nby the lack of buoyancy and fast-moving nature of this system, \nalthough amounts of 0.25-0.50 inches are possible, especially \nnortheast of a line extending from roughly Russell to Newton to \nCoffeyville. \n\nAlso, on the back side of the departing precipitation shield \ntonight, can't completely rule out a few rogue wet snowflakes mixing \nin with the rain, as the entire column will be below freezing less \nthe lowest 1000-1500 ft AGL. Surface wet-bulbs colder than the mid-\nupper 30s are unlikely, so absolutely no impacts or accumulations \nare expected. \n\nLATE WEDNESDAY NIGHT--FRIDAY MORNING...The next chance of meaningful \nrainfall looks to arrive mid-late week, as deterministic and \nensemble consensus progresses another potent shortwave over Mid-\nAmerica. Unseasonably high moisture and weak buoyancy may even \nsupport a few thunderstorms, although severe weather is unlikely. \nRun-to-run and model-to-model consistency is improving with this \nsystem, which prompted increased PoPs. Stay tuned as we continue to \nrefine forecast details in the coming days.",
